%I A369370 #14 Oct 21 2026 00:03:42
%S A369370 0,1,3,15,3,156,15,1284,87,642,172,2189,149,15,2865,215,87
%N A369370 a(n) is the numerator of the maximum expected number of steps until a random walker falls into the sea when walking on the cells of an n-celled polyiamond island.
%C A369370 This is the polyiamond version of A366998.
%C A369370 For all n <= 16 except n = 7, 8, and 15, the optimal island (with a designated starting cell) is unique up to translations, rotations, and reflections. The three exceptional cases all have two optimal islands. For n = 7, the two optimal polyiamonds have the same underlying graph, but that is not the case for n = 8 and n = 15. See linked illustration.
%H A369370 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="/A369370/a369370.svg">Illustration of the optimal islands and starting cells (black dots) for n = 1..16</a>.
%H A369370 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="https://oeis.org/plot2a?name1=A369370&amp;name2=A369371&amp;tform1=untransformed&amp;tform2=untransformed&amp;shift=0&amp;radiop1=ratio&amp;drawpoints=true">Plot of a(n)/A369371(n) vs n</a>, using Plot2.
%H A369370 <a href="/index/Pol#polyominoes">Index entries for sequences related to polyominoes</a>.
%e A369370 For n = 0, the random walker falls into the sea immediately, so a(0) = 0.
%e A369370 For n = 1, the starting cell is the only cell of the island, so the random walker falls into the sea after the first step and a(1) = 1.
%e A369370 For n = 2, the island consists of the starting cell and one adjacent cell, so the probability that the random walker stays on the island is 1/3 at each step. This implies that the expected number of steps is 3/2, so a(2) = 3. (The number of steps follows a geometric distribution.)
%e A369370 For n = 3, the island consists of the starting cell and two of its neighboring cells. Let x be the expected length of the random walk with the given starting cell, and let y be the expected length of a random walk starting at one of the other two cells. By conditioning on the first step, it follows that the equations x = 1 + y*2/3 and y = 1 + x/3 hold, giving x = 15/7 and a(3) = 15.
%e A369370 See linked illustration for optimal solutions for 1 <= n <= 16.
%Y A369370 Cf. A000577, A369371 (denominators), A366998 (polyominoes), A369368 (polyhexes), A397795 (polyknights).
